module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.parsing.counted
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}
import sysl.math.bigint.{BigInt, from_int as big_from_int}
import sysl.text.Search

// What a slate program computes with.
//
// **The `NativeFn` tag is `native_fn.sysl`**, cut out of this file when it passed a thousand lines:
// a closed list of five hundred builtin names is a subject of its own, and what is left here is the
// union, a scope, and the tags an object carries.
//
// slate is dynamically typed, so this is the tagged union every such language has, and the whole of
// its type system lives in the arms that read it.
//
// **Not one member of it is reference counted**, and that is what makes it cheap: scalars, and raw
// pointers into the collected heap. A `Value` copies with no ARC traffic, stores into GC memory
// without a write barrier, and needs no finalizer of its own -- the objects it points at have those.
//
// **An array and an object are reference types because the pointer is the value.** Two names for one
// array see each other's writes, which is what JavaScript, Python and Ruby all do and is not
// something slate implements. A number and a boolean are copied.
enum Value
    Null

    // **Absence, and the one value a program may not keep.**
    //
    // It comes from reading a field an object does not have, and nowhere else. It may be compared,
    // defaulted with `??`, and tested in a condition -- but it may not be bound to a name, stored in
    // a container, or passed to a function, and every one of those is refused where it is attempted.
    //
    // **The point is that absence stops at the boundary it arose at.** JavaScript's `undefined` is an
    // ordinary value, so it flows into a field, through three layers, and is observed a long way from
    // the lookup that made it -- by which time nothing knows what was missing. Here the program has to
    // say what absence means where it happens, which is the one place it still knows.
    Undefined
    Bool(v: bool)
    Int(v: long)

    // An integer that has outgrown 64 bits.
    //
    // **An integer never wraps in slate; it grows**, which is Python's rule, and this is the second
    // half of the one kind `Int` is the first half of. A program cannot tell them apart -- `is
    // integer` answers true for both, `==` compares the numbers, and the only thing the split says
    // is how the number is being held.
    //
    // **A pointer for `Str`'s reason**: a `BigInt` owns a reference-counted view of limbs and a
    // `Value` holds no counted member at all, so what sits here is the cell and what owns the limbs
    // is the cell. `Value` therefore does not grow by one byte.
    //
    // **THE INVARIANT IS THAT A `Big` THAT FITS A `long` NEVER EXISTS.** `big_value` below is the
    // only way one is made and it answers an `Int` wherever the number fits, so `(2^64) - (2^64) + 5`
    // is `Int(5)`, hashes as `Int(5)` and finds a map entry made under `Int(5)`. Without it every
    // comparison and every hash in the language would owe a cross-kind case.
    Big(p: *BigObj)
    Real(v: real)
    Str(p: *StrObj)
    Array(p: *ArrayObj)

    // A run of raw bytes -- what a socket delivers, what a file holds, and what an encoding of a
    // string is.
    //
    // **A reference type for an array's reason: the pointer is the value.** Two names for one buffer
    // see each other's writes, and `push` grows the buffer both of them hold rather than answering a
    // second one.
    //
    // **It is a kind of its own rather than an array of numbers**, which is what slate had until now.
    // The array costs a whole `Value` for each byte and reads every one of them through a tagged
    // union, so encoding a string meant undoing UTF-8 one byte at a time in the interpreter -- and
    // there was no way at all to say "the bytes of this string, in this encoding" or to append one
    // buffer to another. Both are one call here, and neither loops in slate.
    Bytes(p: *BytesObj)
    Object(p: *ObjectObj)
    Fn(p: *ClosureObj)

    // A value that is not there yet, which `await` waits for and an `async` function answers.
    Promise(p: *PromiseObj)

    // `a..b` and `a..<b`.
    //
    // **A cell of its own, because three numbers and three flags would make every `Value` 40 bytes
    // wide** -- the whole union is as wide as its widest variant, and a range is the only one past
    // one word. The cell is `boxed.sysl`'s, made once where the range is written and never changed,
    // so two values naming one cell are the same range and a copy is still a pointer's width. A `for`
    // over a range reads its ends through the pointer once a turn, which costs a load; every push,
    // pop and slot read in every program costing 16 bytes rather than 40 is what that buys.
    //
    // `has_lo` and `has_hi` say which ends were written. An end left out is taken from whatever the
    // range is used on -- zero and the length, for a slice -- and a `for` over a range with no end
    // is refused rather than run forever.
    //
    // **`step` is always set and is `1` for a range nobody gave a `by`**, so every walk multiplies by
    // it rather than asking first. It is never zero: a written zero is refused where it stands and a
    // computed one where it is worked out, so nothing downstream has to guard against a range that
    // would never move.
    RangeVal(p: *RangeObj)

    // A TCP socket -- a listener, or a connection at either end of one.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `event.sysl` keeps, and not the handle itself.** A `&Tcp` is a counted
    // box and a counted box may not live in the collected heap at all (see `obj.sysl`), so what a
    // program holds is the slot and what holds the handle is the slot. That also makes a socket a
    // scalar, which is what keeps this union free of reference counting.
    //
    // **`gen` is why this is two numbers and not one.** A spent slot is reused, so `id` alone names
    // a position rather than a socket: a value held across a `close` would come to mean whatever
    // took the position next, and compare equal to it. The generation is stamped at the claim and
    // never reused, so a closed socket's value matches nothing ever again.
    //
    // **Two 32-bit halves, so the pair is one word** and a `Value` stays two. A table of four
    // billion sockets is not a table anything runs, and a generation that wraps would need four
    // billion claims of one slot while a value from the first was still held.
    Socket(id: u32, gen: u32)

    // A child process this program started and is still talking to -- a slot in the table
    // `spawn.sysl` keeps, and never libuv's handle itself. **A socket's shape, for a socket's
    // reasons**: the handle is a counted box that may not live in the collected heap, and the
    // generation is what stops a value held past the child's last breath from coming to mean the
    // next child to take the slot. Two 32-bit halves for a socket's reason too.
    ChildVal(id: u32, gen: u32)

    // The message channel a child was started with, from the child's own side -- what `channel()`
    // answers. **One number where a child needs two**: a program has one channel for as long as it
    // runs, there is nothing that gives its slot back, and so no position is ever reused.
    ChannelVal(id: usize)

    // A compiled regular expression -- a slot in the table `regex.sysl` keeps, and not the pattern
    // itself. A `&libregexp.Regex` is a counted box, and one may not live in the collected heap.
    //
    // **One number where a socket needs two**, and the difference says what the two tables are. A
    // socket's slot is surrendered by `close` and handed to the next socket, so its value carries a
    // generation to keep a closed socket from coming to mean a live one. A compiled pattern is never
    // released -- there is no `close` for one and a program cannot ask -- so a slot names the same
    // pattern for as long as the program runs, and the id alone is the whole of the value.
    RegexVal(id: usize)

    // A value of the JavaScript host, which an `external` declaration named.
    //
    // **The number is the interned PATH and not a slot in a table**, which is what makes `==`
    // identity with nothing to keep: two declarations of one path are one number, and two paths are
    // two -- the same answer the host gives, where the host is there to ask. Nothing about the value
    // is held here, because in the interpreter there is nothing to hold: every operation that would
    // reach the host faults naming the command, and the declaration itself succeeds so that an
    // import of a module using one is not turned into a complaint about the module.
    External(path: usize)

    // A declared type, as a value -- `type NewNote = { ... }` binds one under its own name.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `shape.sysl` keeps, and one number for `RegexVal`'s reason**: a shape is
    // never released, so a slot names the same shape for as long as the program runs. What the slot
    // holds is the resolved pattern, the name it was declared under, and **the scope it was declared
    // in** -- the last so that a shape naming a class still finds that class wherever the shape is
    // carried to. A pattern is a counted box and a scope is a collected one, so neither may sit in a
    // `Value` and both sit in the table.
    //
    // **The shape itself stays opaque: nothing reads its structure back out.** A program may ask
    // whether a value fits and what does not fit about it, and that is the whole of the surface --
    // which is what keeps "a type has no run-time cost" true in the sense that mattered.
    Shape(id: usize)

    // A point on the timeline, in microseconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.
    //
    // **It names no place and no calendar, which is the whole reason there are eight of these and
    // not one.** JavaScript has a single `Date`, which is an instant that answers calendar questions
    // by silently reading the host's zone -- so `d.getFullYear()` gives a different answer on the
    // developer's machine and on the server, and nothing in the program says which zone it meant.
    // An instant here has no year until a zone is named.
    InstantVal(us: long)

    // An **exact** length of timeline, in microseconds.
    //
    // Not a number of days: a day a zone's clocks move through is 23 or 25 hours long, so a length
    // that means "a day" is a `PeriodVal` and a length that means 24 hours is this. Which one a
    // program wanted is the difference between two right answers, so it is a type rather than a
    // parameter.
    DurationVal(us: long)

    // A calendar day, as a count from 1970-01-01. No time of day and no zone.
    DateVal(day: int)

    // A wall clock reading, in microseconds from midnight. No date and no zone.
    //
    // **It has no arithmetic**, which is deliberate: 23:30 plus an hour is either 00:30 the next day
    // or a fault, and nothing about a bare reading says which was meant. Combine it with a date and
    // the question answers itself.
    TimeVal(us: long)

    // A civil reading -- a date and a time of day -- with no zone.
    //
    // What a person writes on a form and what a calendar entry holds before anybody says where it
    // happens. It names a different instant in every zone, and in one zone twice a year it names
    // either two instants or none.
    //
    // **A cell, for a range's reason**: a day and a time of day are 69 bits between them, one more
    // word than a `Value` has room for. `boxed.sysl` holds it.
    DateTimeVal(p: *DateTimeObj)

    // A zone: a region's rules, or a fixed offset from UTC.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `time.sysl` keeps, and not the rules themselves**, for `Socket`'s reason
    // -- a parsed zone is a struct of counted sequences and a counted box may not live in the
    // collected heap. A zone is interned by name and never released, so unlike a socket there is
    // nothing to reuse and no generation to stamp.
    ZoneVal(id: usize)

    // An instant read in a zone: the type an appointment, a log line and a calendar entry all want.
    //
    // Both halves are needed and neither is enough. The instant is what orders two of them and what
    // a difference is taken between; the zone is what makes it 9 in the morning rather than a number
    // of microseconds, and what makes tomorrow morning still 9 in the morning after the clocks move.
    // A cell, for a range's reason: an instant is a whole word before the zone is counted.
    ZonedVal(p: *ZonedObj)

    // A **calendar** length: whole months and whole days.
    //
    // **The days are not months and never convert**, which is the fact this type exists to keep.
    // A month is 28, 29, 30 or 31 days depending on which one and which year, so `months(1).days()`
    // is zero rather than a guess -- and adding a month to the 31st of January lands on the last day
    // of February rather than on the 3rd of March, which is where JavaScript's `setMonth` puts it.
    PeriodVal(months: int, days: int)

    // A generator: a line of execution a program drives itself, one `yield` at a time.
    //
    // **A slot in the table `generator.sysl` keeps, for `Socket`'s reason** -- what it holds is a
    // parked `Machine`, whose stack and frames are counted buffers that may not live in the
    // collected heap at all. The machine itself sits in `parked`, so the tracer already walks
    // everything the suspended body is holding.
    //
    // **`gen` is here for the reason a socket's is**: a generator that has run to the end gives its
    // slot back, so a bare id would come to mean whatever took the slot next -- and a finished
    // generator would compare equal to a fresh one. Two 32-bit halves for a socket's reason.
    Gen(id: u32, gen: u32)

    // A collection of values, each at most once, in the order they were added.
    //
    // **It points at an `ObjectObj`, which is already a hash table keyed by any value at all.** An
    // object's keys and a set's members are the same problem -- an insertion-ordered table whose
    // keys are hashed structurally and compared with `==`, consulting a class's own `hash` and `==`
    // where it wrote them -- so a second table would be a copy of `table.sysl` differing in nothing
    // but the name. What a set does with the value half is ignore it.
    SetVal(p: *ObjectObj)

    // A table from any value to any value, in the order the keys were first written.
    //
    // **`Object` and this are the same storage and not the same thing**, which is the distinction
    // JavaScript draws with its own `Map`. An object is a record: its keys are the program's field
    // names, it carries a `proto`, and `o.x` is a lookup a reader expects to be a field. A map is a
    // container: its keys are data, nothing about it is a field, and a key that happens to spell
    // `proto` is a key like any other.
    MapVal(p: *ObjectObj)

    // A table whose keys do not keep their entries alive.
    //
    // **The same `ObjectObj` a map is, allocated under a different `Kind`** -- so the hashing, the
    // probing and the insertion order are one implementation and what differs is the three
    // collector hooks the kind carries: `trace` marks neither half, `ephemeron` marks a value
    // *because* its key turned out live, and `weaken` drops the entries whose keys did not. That is
    // the whole of what makes this weak, and it is why a weak map cannot be a map with a flag on it.
    WeakMapVal(p: *ObjectObj)

    // A hold on a value that does not keep it alive.
    //
    // **Its own object, because what it holds is one slot rather than a table.** `trace` marks
    // nothing and `weaken` nulls the slot once marking has decided the target is dead, which is the
    // definition of a weak reference stated in the two hooks the collector already has.
    WeakRefVal(p: *WeakRefObj)

    // A handle on an actor -- a thread with a whole slate runtime on it.
    //
    // **A slot in the process-wide table `actor_run.sysl` keeps, and never the mailbox itself.** A
    // `&sync ActorBox` is a counted box and a counted box may not live in the collected heap at all
    // (see `obj.sysl`), so what a program holds is the number -- which also makes a handle a scalar,
    // and therefore something that crosses to a third actor as itself.
    //
    // **`gen` is why this is two numbers and not one**, and it is `Socket`'s reason: a slot is reused,
    // so an id alone would name a *position* and a dead actor's handle would come to mean whatever
    // took the position next. A dead actor's handle stays a value -- it prints, it compares, it can
    // be stored -- and what it cannot do is deliver. Two 32-bit halves for a socket's reason.
    ActorVal(id: u32, gen: u32)

    // An actor and one of its handlers, which is what reading a handler's name off a handle answers.
    //
    // **A message is a VALUE**, so it can itself be sent -- which is how a reply address travels.
    // `c.total` sends nothing and asks nothing; it names an actor and one of its handlers, and `send`
    // and `ask` are the two things that can be done with one.
    //
    // **The name is interned per VM for `RegexVal`'s reason**: a `Value` holds no reference-counted
    // member, so what travels here is a number into `message_names` and the text is written out when
    // the message crosses.
    //
    // **Packed into one word, the generation first so the three align without a gap.** A slot
    // number is under `MaxActors`, 1,024, and a VM that has interned 65,536 handler names is refused
    // the next one by `message_name_id` in as many words -- the list is searched from the front, so a
    // program anywhere near that many would have stopped for other reasons first.
    MessageVal(gen: u32, id: u16, name: u16)

    // A function the interpreter supplies.
    //
    // **An ID into the registry rather than the function itself, so that a `Value` stays free of
    // reference counting.** A callable in a concrete slot is `&Fn(…)`, which is a counted box, and a
    // `string` for its name would be the one ARC member in the union -- either would put a finalizer
    // on every object that can hold a value. `native_fn.sysl` holds the table the id indexes, and an
    // integer compares and hashes exactly as the closed enum this replaced did.
    Native(f: NativeFn)

    // A function a C host registered with `slate_register` (`embed_host.sysl`).
    //
    // **An index into the VM's own `host_fns`, not a `Native`**, because the builtin registry is
    // process-wide and append-only -- a function one embedding registers would be a builtin in every
    // VM in the process, and a second registration of one name would have nowhere to go. A handle's
    // host functions are that handle's, and they die with its VM.
    HostFn(id: usize)

    // The name a program uses for this kind of value, which is what a runtime complaint says.
    type_name(self) -> string = self match
        Null -> "null"
        Undefined -> "undefined"
        Bool(_) -> "a boolean"
        Int(_) -> "an integer"

        // **IT SAYS THE WIDTH, AND THAT ONE PHRASE IS WHAT SERVES EVERY CALL THAT NEEDS A MACHINE
        // INTEGER.** An index, a `repeat` count, a byte, a port and a number of milliseconds are all
        // read by a site that matches `Int(n)` and falls through to a sentence built out of
        // `type_name()`. By the invariant above a `Big` is *always* too wide for one of those, so
        // naming the width here gives about forty such sites the right sentence -- "an array index is
        // a whole number, and this is an integer too large to fit 64 bits" -- with no arm of their
        // own. Answering "an integer" would have sent every one of those readers to stare at a value
        // that is exactly the kind the call asked for.
        Big(_) -> "an integer too large to fit 64 bits"
        Real(_) -> "a real"
        Str(_) -> "a string"
        Array(_) -> "an array"

        // **No article, because the word is plural.** "this is bytes" is the sentence every
        // complaint about one reads, and "a bytes" would be the one entry here that does not.
        Bytes(_) -> "bytes"
        Object(_) -> "an object"
        Fn(_) -> "a function"
        Promise(_) -> "a promise"
        RangeVal(_) -> "a range"
        Socket(_, _) -> "a socket"
        ChildVal(_, _) -> "a child process"
        ChannelVal(_) -> "a channel"
        RegexVal(_) -> "a regular expression"
        External(_) -> "an external"
        Shape(_) -> "a shape"
        InstantVal(_) -> "an instant"
        DurationVal(_) -> "a duration"
        DateVal(_) -> "a date"
        TimeVal(_) -> "a time"
        DateTimeVal(_) -> "a dateTime"
        ZoneVal(_) -> "a zone"
        ZonedVal(_) -> "a zoned"
        PeriodVal(_, _) -> "a period"
        Gen(_, _) -> "a generator"
        SetVal(_) -> "a set"
        MapVal(_) -> "a map"
        WeakMapVal(_) -> "a weak map"
        WeakRefVal(_) -> "a weak reference"
        ActorVal(_, _) -> "an actor"
        MessageVal(_, _, _) -> "a message"
        Native(_) -> "a function"
        HostFn(_) -> "a function"

    // Whether this counts as true in a condition.
    //
    // **This is JavaScript's rule.** `false`, `null`, an absent (`undefined`) value, `0` (and `-0`),
    // `NaN` and `""` are false; everything else is true -- including `[]`, `{}`, `"0"`, `" "`, a
    // function and an empty `bytes` buffer, which is an object in JS terms and stays true regardless
    // of its length.
    truthy(self) -> bool = self match
        Null -> false
        Undefined -> false
        Bool(v) -> v
        Int(v) -> v != 0

        // A `Big` is never zero -- zero fits a `long` -- so it is always true. Written out rather
        // than left to the default arm, because the reason is the invariant and not the kind.
        Big(_) -> true
        Real(v) -> v != 0.0 && v == v
        Str(p) -> p.s != ""
        _ -> true
end Value

// The text of a slate string, for the interpreter's own use.
text_of(p: *StrObj) -> string = p.s

// A slate string value over a sysl string whose character count nobody knows. The GC object becomes
// that string's only owner.
//
// **THIS ONE WALKS THE TEXT TO COUNT IT, so reach for `str_counted` wherever the number is already
// in hand** -- which it nearly always is, a new string being made out of strings that were already
// counted. See `new_str_counted`.
str_value(s: string) -> Value = Str(new_str_scanned(s))

// The same, where the caller knows how many characters it is handing over.
str_counted(s: string, chars: usize) -> Value = Str(new_str_counted(s, chars))

// A slate bytes value over a `Buf[u8]`, which the GC object becomes the only owner of.
bytes_value(b: Buf[u8]) -> Value = Bytes(new_bytes(b))

// **THE ONE WAY A BIG INTEGER BECOMES A VALUE, AND IT NORMALIZES.** Every arm of every operation
// that could grow a number answers through this, so a result back inside 64 bits comes out as an
// `Int` and the `Big` variant means exactly one thing: too wide.
//
// See `Value.Big` for why the rest of the language depends on that.
big_value(n: BigInt) -> Value = n.to_long() match
    Some(v) -> Int(v)
    None -> Big(new_big(n))

// The number a value holds, for a caller that has already decided both sides are whole.
//
// **`Int` costs one limb and `Big` costs nothing**, which is the arrangement `sysl.math.bigint`'s
// own page argues for: a program over this type nearly always has one large number and one small
// one, so the small-number forms (`add_long`, `cmp_long`) are reached where only one side grew and
// this is for the case where both are read alike.
as_big(v: Value) -> BigInt = v match
    Big(p) -> p.n
    Int(n) -> big_from_int(n)
    _ -> big_from_int(0)

// Whether a value is a whole number, of either width.
whole_value(v: Value) -> bool = v match
    Int(_) -> true
    Big(_) -> true
    _ -> false

// -- scopes -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a name holds, looked up outward through the chain.
lookup(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> Option[Value]
    var scope = e

    // A loop rather than the recursion this reads as, because a scope chain is as deep as a
    // program's nesting and a walk up it is a walk up a linked list.
    loop
        if scope == null then return None

        scope.names.get(name) match
            Some(v) -> return Some(v)
            None -> scope = scope.parent
end lookup

// Whether a name is bound anywhere in the chain.
knows(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> bool = lookup(e, name).is_some()

// Whether a name is bound in THIS scope, without looking outward.
//
// **The distinction is what a default parameter turns on.** A call's scope holds exactly the
// arguments that were given, so asking about this scope alone answers "was it given"; asking the
// chain would find a global of the same name and answer yes to a parameter nobody passed.
bound_here(e: *EnvObj, name: string) -> bool = e.names.has(name)

// Bind a name in this scope, shadowing whatever an outer one had.
//
// **The two tables are payload**, so a scope that rehashes to hold another name is charged for the
// table it grew -- a program with many long-lived scopes holds most of its storage here.
declare(e: *EnvObj, name: string, v: Value, assignable: bool)
    val was = env_payload(e)

    e.names.put(name, v)
    e.mutable.put(name, assignable)
    env_recharged(e, was)

// Write to an existing binding, wherever in the chain it lives.
//
// Three answers rather than two: the name may be unknown, or known and not writable, and the two are
// different complaints.
assign_name(e: *EnvObj, name: string, v: Value) -> Assigned
    var scope = e

    loop
        if scope == null then return Unknown

        if scope.names.has(name)
            if !scope.mutable.get_or(name, false) then return Immutable

            scope.names.put(name, v)
            return Written
        end if

        scope = scope.parent
end assign_name

// What happened when a name was written to.
enum Assigned
    Written
    Immutable
    Unknown

// The field a data variant's maker carries its own name under.
//
// **Unspellable on purpose**, as a constructor's hidden binding is: it sits on the maker rather than
// on the value, so an instance finds it up the proto chain and pays nothing for it, and no program
// can read or write it by name. It exists so that a data value can print as what made it -- an
// object cannot otherwise say which variant it is, the maker not knowing its own name either.
const DataTag: string = "(data)"

// The name a CLASS declaration writes on the class object, by which a value made from it knows what
// it is.
//
// **The same arrangement `DataTag` uses, for the same two reasons**: it sits on the class rather
// than on the instance, so an instance finds it up the proto chain, carries nothing itself, and it
// stays out of `keys`, out of `==` and out of a `with` copy. It is unspellable, so no program can
// forge one.
//
// **It exists because nothing downstream of the parser knew that classes existed**, which was right
// while a class was sugar for an object and stopped being right the day a class instance got an
// equality of its own. `class_name` reads it and two questions rest on the answer: whether `==` on
// this value means identity, and what name its `toString` prints.
const ClassTag: string = "(class)"

// The field a `class` or `data` declaration writes its own SHAPE under.
//
// **The third of the unspellable tags, and it is on the same terms as the other two**: it sits on
// the class or the variant maker, out of `keys`, out of `==`, out of a `with` copy and out of what a
// program can write. An instance reaches it up the chain and pays nothing for it.
//
// **It is what makes a class name answer `test`, `mismatch` and `name`**, which the types page has
// always said a class name is -- `docs/reference/types.md` lists "a `type`, `class` or `data` name"
// as a type expression, and until this the value half of those two was a plain object with no shape
// near it. `read_field` reaches this only where the object's own fields and its whole proto chain
// have said nothing, so a static the program itself declared wins.
const ShapeTag: string = "(shape)"

// The keys a `get` and a `set` member are stored under, the property's own name after them.
//
// **A PROPERTY IS A FIELD OF THE CLASS UNDER A NAME NO PROGRAM CAN WRITE**, which is the arrangement
// the three tags above already use: `get area(self)` puts a function on the class at `(get)area`, so
// an instance reaches it up the proto chain, carries nothing itself, and a subclass overrides one by
// writing another. Nothing new is stored on an object and no field lookup changed shape.
//
// **The name is kept whole after the marker** rather than encoded, so `accessor_prop` is a slice and
// a diagnostic has the word the reader wrote.
const GetPrefix: string = "(get)"

const SetPrefix: string = "(set)"

// The key a class writes to say that one of its members was declared `val`, the member's own name
// after it.
//
// **A `val` member is one value the class carries and a `var` is a field each object gets**, so
// `p.x = 2` on a `val` is a program writing a field that shadows the class's value -- the read after
// it answers 2 and the class still answers 1, which is the mistake this marker exists to refuse.
// There is nowhere on the VALUE to record that it does not change, the value being whatever the
// program wrote; the marker goes on the class beside it, where `(get)` and `(set)` already are, and
// a write walks to it exactly as a property lookup does.
const ValPrefix: string = "(val)"

// The field an `actor` declaration writes to say it is one, and the field holding the names it wrote
// `on` in front of.
//
// **The fourth and fifth unspellable tags, on the terms the other three already have**: both sit on
// the declaration rather than on an instance, out of `keys`, out of `==`, out of a `with` copy and
// out of what a program can write.
//
// **`ActorTag` is what `spawn` reads to tell an actor from a class**, an actor otherwise being a
// class exactly -- state is `val` and `var` fields, a handler is written in the ordinary definition
// syntax, and the generated constructor takes every field. **`HandlerTag` is what the drain reads**,
// so that a message may name a handler and not an ordinary method: a method a handler calls is the
// actor's own business and is not something another thread may reach.
const ActorTag: string = "(actor)"

const HandlerTag: string = "(handlers)"

// The field `transfer(b)` wraps a buffer in, so that the copier knows to move the storage rather
// than to copy it.
//
// **A tagged object rather than a kind of its own**, because what `transfer` marks is one use of one
// value at one call: the buffer is an ordinary `bytes` everywhere else, and a wrapper that never
// reaches a `send` does nothing at all.
const TransferTag: string = "(transfer)"

// Whether this key holds an accessor rather than a field a program wrote.
accessor_key(s: string) -> bool = s.starts_with(GetPrefix) || s.starts_with(SetPrefix)

// Whether this key is a class's record that a member was declared `val`.
//
// **The first byte is asked before the prefix is**, because every new key of every object comes here
// (`obj_put`) and `starts_with` is a call: a key that does not open with `(` -- which is every key a
// program writes -- is answered by one load and one comparison.
val_key(s: string) -> bool = s.bytes.len > 0 && s.bytes[0] == 40u8 && s.starts_with(ValPrefix)

// The property an accessor key is about. Meaningless for anything `accessor_key` says no to.
accessor_prop(s: string) -> string = s[GetPrefix.len..]

// Whether a key is one of the tags a declaration wrote rather than a field a program did.
//
// **A tag is not a field and nothing that enumerates fields may report one.** `keys`, `values`,
// `entries` and `len` all walk the table's own entries, so without this a class object answers
// `["(class)", "new"]` to `keys` -- a name no program wrote, cannot have written, and would have to
// filter out of every walk over a class. `print` has hidden them from the start and these four had
// not caught up.
//
// **An accessor's key is one of these for the same reason**: `(get)area` is the mechanism behind
// `c.area` and not a field anybody wrote, so a walk over a class reporting it would be reporting the
// spelling rather than the property.
//
// Not `private`: the three walks are in `table.sysl`, `array.sysl` and `builtin.sysl`, and three
// copies of one two-name list is how one of them comes to disagree.
tag_key(k: Value) -> bool = k match
    Str(p) -> p.s == DataTag || p.s == ClassTag || p.s == ShapeTag || p.s == ActorTag ||
        p.s == HandlerTag || p.s == TransferTag || accessor_key(p.s) || val_key(p.s)
    _ -> false

// What to say when a call gives the wrong number of arguments.
//
// **A function with a default parameter takes a RANGE**, and saying only the upper end of it would
// send a reader looking for the argument they left out on purpose. Where there is no default the
// two ends are the same number and the sentence is the one slate has always said.
// **A REST PARAMETER HAS NO UPPER END AT ALL**, so a call cannot give too many and the only thing
// left to say is the floor. Naming an upper end there would be naming a number the function does not
// have.
// **IT NAMES THE FUNCTION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT A READER NEEDS FROM IT.** The caret is on
// the CALL, and a call is very often somewhere the reader has never opened: a framework invoking a
// component, a sort given a comparison, a router handed a handler. *"this function takes 0 arguments
// and was given 1"* pointed at `lath.slx` for a mistake in the reader's own `Counter`, and there was
// nothing in the sentence to grep for. `` `Counter` takes 0 arguments and was given 1 `` sends them
// to the one word that is wrong.
//
// **An anonymous function keeps the old wording**, there being nothing to name: a lambda written at
// the call site is already where the caret is pointing.
// How a diagnostic refers to the function it is about.
//
// **A LAMBDA'S CHUNK IS NAMED `a function`, WHICH IS WHAT `print` CALLS ONE** -- so a message that
// interpolated it said `` `a function` takes 1 argument ``, quoting a phrase as though it were a
// name and inviting the reader to grep for it. A lambda, a method and a generated `new` are all
// compiled as lambdas, so all three land here; none of them has a name to give, and the caret is
// already on the call.
//
// Not `private`: `execute.sysl` says it about named arguments as well as about counts.
callee_named(name: string) -> string =
    if name == "" || name == "a function" then "this function" else s"`${name}`"

// What to say when an object is called and has no `new` to call.
//
// **The reader has to be told that calling an object is a real thing before being told this one
// cannot be**, or the sentence reads as "objects are not callable" and sends them to rewrite a call
// that was right. What is wrong is the object, which is missing the field the call would have used.
no_maker() -> string =
    "an object is called only where it has a `new`, and this one has none -- `class` and `data` write one, and an ordinary object is data rather than something to call"
